"## What is a 'factual claim' in logic and argumentation theory — definition, how to identify the main factual claim in a question or statement\n## Definition\n\nA **factual claim** is a proposition asserting that something *is, was, or will be* the case — a descriptive statement whose truth or falsity is, at least in principle, determinable by evidence, observation, or checking against the world [1][5]. In standard logic, every argument is built from claims: **premises** (claims offered in support) and a **conclusion** (the claim being supported) [1][2]. Toulmin's model calls the conclusion the \"claim\" — the assertion whose merits the argument seeks to establish [3]. In pragma-dialectical argumentation theory, a factual claim functions as a **standpoint**: an externalized position on a descriptive proposition that the arguer must defend if challenged [4].\n\n**Key features:**\n- **Truth-apt** — capable of being true or false. Questions, commands, and exclamations are not claims; only declarative statements are [1][2].\n- **Descriptive, not normative** — \"is\" vs. \"ought.\" \"Unemployment is 8%\" is factual; \"unemployment ought to be lower\" is a value/policy claim [5][6].\n- **Verifiable in principle** — predictions, historical statements, and causal claims count as factual even when hard to confirm [5][7].\n- **Not the same as \"true\"** — a false statement is still a factual claim; \"factual\" describes the *kind* of claim (about how things are), not its accuracy [1][5].\n\nNot factual claims: definitions/conceptual truths (\"a triangle has three sides\"), value judgments, and non-propositional utterances [5][6].\n\n## Identifying the main factual claim\n\n**In a statement or argument:**\n1. Ask: *\"What single point is the author trying to get me to accept?\"* — that is the main claim/conclusion [2][5].\n2. Use **indicator words**: conclusion indicators (*therefore, thus, so, consequently, it follows that*) mark the main claim; premise indicators (*because, since, given that*) mark supporting claims [2].\n3. **Structural test**: the main claim is the statement supported by other statements in the passage, but which itself supports nothing else there [2][6].\n4. **\"Because\" test**: without indicators, try inserting \"because\" between candidate statements in both orders — the sensible arrangement reveals conclusion vs. premises [2].\n5. Screen out background information, examples, restatements, and subsidiary claims [5].\n\n**In a question:**\n- Questions assert nothing, but they typically **presuppose** factual claims. Extract the main claim by identifying what the question takes for granted, or what proposition would answer it [1][7].\n- **Loaded/complex questions** (\"Have you stopped cheating on exams?\") embed a presupposed factual claim — \"you have cheated\" — that must be isolated and evaluated before the question can be answered [1][7].\n- Convert the question into an **issue**, then state rival answers as propositions: \"Is X true?\" → the factual claim at issue is \"X is true\" (or \"X is not true\") [5][6].\n\n## Common pitfalls\n- Confusing a factual claim with an established fact — claims can be disputed, false, or unverifiable [1][5].\n- **Mixed sentences**: \"The law reduced crime\" (factual) vs. \"The law is a success\" (evaluative) — separate the descriptive component being argued [6][7].\n- Mistaking the *topic* for the *claim*: the issue is the question; the claim is a specific answer to it [5][6].\n\n**Sources:** [1] Copi, Cohen & Rodych, *Introduction to Logic*; [2] Hurley & Watson, *A Concise Introduction to Logic*; [3] Toulmin, *The Uses of Argument*; [4] van Eemeren & Grootendorst, *A Systematic Theory of Argumentation*; [5] Bassham et al., *Critical Thinking: A Student's Introduction*; [6] Govier, *A Practical Study of Argument*; [7] Walton, *Fundamentals of Critical Argumentation*.\n\n---\n\n## How English Wikipedia's search works and best practices for selecting key search terms / article titles (Wikipedia search, article naming conventions)\n# How English Wikipedia Search Works & Title/Search-Term Best Practices\n\n*Note: I don't have live browsing in this session; the summary below reflects Wikimedia's canonical documentation pages, cited by index at the end. Verify details on-page if you need the latest syntax.*\n\n## 1. How the search engine works\n\n- **Backend:** English Wikipedia uses **CirrusSearch**, built on Elasticsearch. Edits are indexed near-real-time (usually within seconds to a few minutes) [1][2].\n- **What is searched:** Current revisions of pages in selected namespaces. The default profile covers articles plus some project namespaces; other profiles (\"General,\" \"Images,\" \"Everything,\" \"Advanced\") broaden or narrow scope. Deleted pages and page history are **not** searched [1][2].\n- **Search box (autocomplete):** As you type, suggestions are drawn from **page titles and redirects**, ranked largely by pageviews. Pressing **Enter (\"Go\")** takes you directly to an exact title match or a redirect; otherwise you get a full results page [1][2].\n- **Relevance ranking:** Combines BM25 text scoring with boosts for **title matches, incoming links, and pageviews** [2].\n- **Text normalization:** Search is **case-insensitive**, folds **diacritics** (José ≈ Jose), ignores most **punctuation**, and applies **English stemming** (\"swimming\" matches \"swim\"). Common **stopwords** (\"the,\" \"of,\" \"and\") are dropped in normal queries [1][2].\n\n## 2. Search syntax quick reference [1][2]\n\n| Feature | Syntax | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Exact phrase | `\"Lord of the Rings\"` | Matched literally; stemming disabled |\n| Exclude | `-term` or `NOT term` | Removes results containing term |\n| Alternatives | `term1 OR term2` | Default between words is AND |\n| Grouping | `(a OR b) term` | |\n| Fuzzy spelling | `word~` | 1–2 typos tolerated |\n| Wildcard | `prefix*` | Suffix expansion only |\n| Title contains | `intitle:word` | |\n| Wikitext contains | `insource:word` / `insource:/regex/` | Regex is slow/limited |\n| Category | `incategory:\"X\"` / `deepcategory:` | |\n| Template | `hastemplate:\"X\"` | |\n| Links to page | `linksto:\"X\"` | |\n| Namespace | `namespace:` or `All:` | Also via Advanced Search UI [10] |\n| Proximity | `\"a b\"~3` | Words within N positions |\n\nThe **Special:Search** page adds filters (sort by date/title, templates, file type) and shows a \"create this page\" link when no exact match exists [1][10]. **Special:MediaSearch** handles image search via structured data [2].\n\n## 3. Best practices for choosing search terms\n\n1. **Try the likely title first** — Enter takes you straight there if it (or a redirect) exists [1].\n2. **Use 2–4 distinctive keywords**; skip filler words, which are discarded anyway [1].\n3. **Don't worry about capitalization or accents** — both are normalized [2].\n4. **Quote exact phrases** for names, titles, and quotations [2].\n5. **Exclude noise** with `-term`; broaden with `OR` and synonyms (common vs. technical names — both are often redirected).\n6. **Use fuzzy (`~`) or wildcards (`*`)** when unsure of spelling [2].\n7. **Fall back to disambiguation pages**: searching the ambiguous term surfaces the DAB page listing all uses [7].\n8. **Search other namespaces** (Help, Talk, etc.) via the Advanced tab or `namespace:` [1][10].\n9. If nothing turns up, **reduce keywords** and check the \"Did you mean\" suggestion [1].\n\n## 4. Article title conventions (for editors)\n\nPer the **Article titles policy** (WP:AT), titles should be [3]:\n\n- **Recognizable** — the name most commonly used in reliable sources (WP:COMMONNAME: \"Bill Clinton,\" not the full legal name) [3].\n- **Natural** — what readers are likely to type/look for [3].\n- **Precise** — unambiguous; disambiguate with natural wording (\"Pittsburgh Steelers\") or parentheses (\"Mercury (planet)\") when needed [3][7].\n- **Concise** and **Consistent** with existing conventions [3].\n\nAdditional rules [3][4][5]:\n\n- **Sentence case** — capitalize only the first word and proper nouns (\"Water pollution in New Zealand\") [5].\n- **Singular** unless the term is always plural or a plural proper name (\"Dog\" but \"The Beatles\"); lists use \"List of X\" [4].\n- **Nouns or noun phrases**; no leading \"The\" unless part of the proper name [3].\n- **Avoid abbreviations** unless more recognizable (\"IBM,\" \"NASA\" are fine) [3].\n- **Technical constraints:** ≤255 bytes; no `# < > [ ] | { }`; first letter is forced uppercase (override with `{{lowercase title}}` for cases like \"iCarly\") [3].\n\n**Findability tips:** Titles are unique — one article per exact name, with the **primary topic** holding the bare title [3][7]. Since search and autocomplete index **redirect titles**, create redirects for synonyms, abbreviations, and common misspellings (WP:Redirect) [6]. Renaming is done by **moving** the page (policy-governed for contested moves), which automatically leaves a redirect [8].\n\n## Sources\n\n[1] Help:Searching — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Searching\n[2] Help:CirrusSearch — https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Help:CirrusSearch\n[3] Wikipedia:Article titles — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Article_titles\n[4] Wikipedia:Naming conventions (plurals) — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Naming_conventions_(plurals)\n[5] Wikipedia:Naming conventions (capitalization) — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Naming_conventions_(capitalization)\n[6] Wikipedia:Redirect — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Redirect\n[7] Wikipedia:Disambiguation — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Disambiguation\n[8] Wikipedia:Moving a page — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Moving_a_page\n[9] Wikipedia:Naming conventions (overview) — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Naming_conventions\n[10] Help:Extension:AdvancedSearch — https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Help:Extension:AdvancedSearch\n\n---\n\n## Wikipedia's Verifiability and No Original Research policies — role of Wikipedia in fact-checking and identifying claims\n# Wikipedia's Verifiability & No Original Research Policies: Fact-Checking Role\n\n*Summary reflects the long-standing, stable text of both policies (in force in essentially current form since ~2005–2006). Policy wording may have evolved since my last reliable knowledge update.*\n\n## 1. The Core Policy Framework\n\nWikipedia's content is governed by three core content policies: Neutral Point of View, Verifiability, and No Original Research [1][2][4].\n\n**Verifiability (WP:V)** [1]\n- Readers and editors \"must be able to check that information comes from a reliable published source.\" All quotations and any material that is challenged or likely to be challenged require inline citations.\n- Threshold principle: **\"verifiability, not truth\"** — inclusion turns on whether a claim has been published in a reliable source, not on an editor's personal belief. However, this does not mean Wikipedia knowingly keeps falsehoods; conflicting reliable sources are reported with attribution, and verifiable material may still be excluded for due-weight reasons.\n- **Burden of evidence (WP:BURDEN)**: the editor who adds or restores challenged material bears responsibility for sourcing it; unsourced challenged content can be removed immediately.\n- **Reliable sources (WP:RS/WP:IRS)** [3]: published works with a reputation for fact-checking and editorial oversight; peer-reviewed scholarship is preferred for academic claims. Self-published sources are generally barred, with narrow exceptions (recognized experts writing independently; limited \"about-self\" use per WP:ABOUTSELF). Sources found unreliable are formally \"deprecated\" (e.g., the *Daily Mail* in 2017) [11].\n\n**No Original Research (WP:NOR)** [2]\n- Articles must not contain material — facts, allegations, ideas, analyses — for which no reliable published source exists.\n- **Synthesis (WP:SYNTH)**: combining reliable sources to reach or imply a conclusion the sources themselves do not state is prohibited, even if each source is individually reliable.\n- **Source hierarchy (WP:PSTS)** [2][3]: primary sources may be used only cautiously, for plain descriptive facts; secondary sources are preferred for interpretation and analysis.\n- Narrow allowances: routine arithmetic (WP:CALC) and faithful translations of source text are not considered original research.\n\n## 2. Role in Fact-Checking and Claim Identification\n\n- **Traceability infrastructure**: Inline citations make every claim traceable to a published source, which is why journalists and professional fact-checkers routinely use Wikipedia as a *starting point* to locate primary sources — Wikipedia itself does not adjudicate truth, it attributes claims [1][2].\n- **Claim flagging**: \"Citation needed\" tags and projects like WikiProject Fact and Reference Check (WP:FACT) systematically mark unsourced assertions [1][5].\n- **Curated claim lists**: Resources such as the *List of common misconceptions* are widely used to identify recurring false claims [6].\n- **Platform integration**: YouTube (2018) began surfacing Wikipedia snippets alongside conspiracy-theory videos; Google's Knowledge Graph draws on Wikipedia; the WHO–Wikimedia Foundation partnership (October 2020) routed COVID-19 queries to Wikipedia content [9][12].\n- **Accuracy evidence**: Nature's 2005 comparison found Wikipedia's science articles roughly comparable to Encyclopædia Britannica in error rate (~4 vs. ~3 errors per reviewed article) [8].\n\n## 3. Limitations for Fact-Checking\n\n- **Verifiability ≠ truth**: Wikipedia mirrors the published record, which can itself be wrong, outdated, or biased [1].\n- **Circular reporting (WP:CIRCULAR)**: sources citing Wikipedia that is citing those sources can launder claims; Wikipedia explicitly cannot cite itself [7].\n- **Link rot**: analyses from 2014–2016 found roughly half of English Wikipedia articles contained at least one dead external link, prompting Internet Archive partnership to preserve citations [10].\n- **Coverage and systemic bias** in what gets sourced and verified at all [1][3].\n\n## Sources\n\n[1] Wikipedia:Verifiability (policy) — en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Verifiability\n[2] Wikipedia:No original research (policy, incl. WP:SYNTH, WP:PSTS, WP:CALC) — en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:No_original_research\n[3] Wikipedia:Reliable sources / Identifying reliable sources (guidelines, incl. SPS/ABOUTSELF)\n[4] Wikipedia:Neutral point of view (policy)\n[5] Wikipedia:Citing sources & WikiProject Fact and Reference Check\n[6] Wikipedia:List of common misconceptions\n[7] Wikipedia:Circular reporting essay (WP:CIRCULAR)\n[8] Giles, J., \"Internet encyclopaedias go head to head,\" *Nature* 438, 900–901 (2005)\n[9] Wikimedia Foundation–WHO partnership announcement (22 Oct 2020)\n[10] Internet Archive/Wikimedia link-rot analyses (2014–2016)\n[11] Wikipedia:Deprecated sources (Daily Mail RfC, June 2017)\n[12] Press coverage of YouTube–Wikipedia info boxes (March 2018)"